-- title:
--   Prolongation of a K-rational place of K' to F'
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $F'$ a $K'$-algebra, $K'$ a $K$-algebra and $F'$ an $F$-algebra, the two towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$ being compatible with a given $K$-algebra structure on $F'$, and assume $K$ is algebraically closed. Assume further that $F$ contains an element transcendental over $K$ over whose generated subfield $F$ is finite-dimensional, that $F'$ contains an element transcendental over $K'$ over whose generated subfield $F'$ is finite-dimensional, and that the subfield of $F'$ generated by $K'$ together with the image of $F$ is all of $F'$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $K'$ containing the image of $K$, and let $\sigma \colon A \to K$ be a ring homomorphism whose kernel is the maximal ideal of $A$ and which sends the image of each $c \in K$ back to $c$. Then there exist a valuation subring $\mathcal O$ of $F'$ and a ring homomorphism $\rho \colon \mathcal O \to F$ such that: the image in $F'$ of every $f \in F$ lies in $\mathcal O$ and is mapped by $\rho$ to $f$; the kernel of $\rho$ is the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$; for every nonzero $f' \in F'$ there is $c \in K'$ with $c f' \in \mathcal O$ and $\rho(c f') \neq 0$; and for every $c \in K'$ whose image in $F'$ lies in $\mathcal O$, the value $\rho(c)$ lies in the image of $K$ in $F$.
--
--   This is the prolongation step of Deuring's constant-reduction theory: a $K$-rational place of the constant field $K'$, presented as a valuation subring $A$ with a retraction onto $K$, is extended to a place of the one-variable function field $F' = K' \cdot F$ whose residue map restricts to the identity on $F$, is unramified in the sense that every nonzero element of $F'$ can be scaled by a constant to a unit-like element, and carries constants to constants. It feeds into [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_constantReduction_of_constantFieldExtension`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_constantReduction_of_constantFieldExtension).
